# Declaration kinds collected by the BY-VALUE checker spine and NOT by the
# *mut transcription that the default engine (Madaros) actually runs.
#
# Each line is a language feature that may be INERT on the default engine.
# "May be", not "is": some of these are plausibly handled by a later pass
# rather than by the collect pass, and this ledger does not claim to have
# checked each one. What it does claim is that nobody has checked, and that
# is exactly the state that let `algebra` and `unit` sit dead for months.
#
# THE RULE: this list may SHRINK. It may not grow. A new divergence fails
# scripts/ci/checker_spine_parity_gate.sh. Removing a line means either the
# arm was wired into checker_collect_item_inplace, or someone established
# where it is handled instead and recorded that in the commit.
#
# Measured 2026-09-09 against self-hosted/check/check.sio:
#   by-value collect_item          28 ItemKind arms
#   checker_collect_item_inplace   10 ItemKind arms
#
# Two entries were on this list until this commit and are not any more:
#   ItemAlgebra  wired 2026-09-01, after `algebra {}` was found inert
#   ItemUnit     wired 2026-09-09, after units of measure were found inert
#                (11 of 14 unit fixtures failed on Madaros, 14 of 14 passed
#                on lean_single)
#
#   ItemOntology wired 2026-09-09, after the whole subsystem was found inert
#
# ItemOntology was the one this ledger called confirmed-broken, and the note
# it carried is kept below because what that failed attempt measured is the
# reason the second one worked.
#
#   Wiring the STRUCTURAL layer alone -- class registration, superclasses,
#   property refinements, disjointness, closure, and binding class names into
#   the type environment -- took the ontology fixtures from 40 of 42 passing
#   to 11. ontology_inference_query_subclass reads the KERNEL structures,
#   which only ontology_inference_collect_kernel populates, so wiring the
#   class table without the kernel makes every subsumption query answer false.
#
#   And that explains why those 40 fixtures were green to begin with: with
#   ItemOntology uncollected, ontology_id was never set on any type, so every
#   ontology boundary check early-returned and passed. THE SUBSYSTEM WAS
#   INERT AND THE TESTS WERE PASSING BECAUSE OF IT.
#
#   Reaching the kernel through a by-value bridge is not an option either:
#   the Checker is ~8 MB and copying it onto the stack segfaults regardless of
#   call depth. That was tried and produced SIGSEGV on 40 of the 42 fixtures.
#
# Closing it took the axiom layer as well -- ontology_inference_collect_kernel,
# its four collectors and the two validation passes -- landed at once, plus
# three defects that only appear once the subsystem actually runs. Each was
# measured before it was fixed, and none of the three was the cause guessed
# beforehand:
#
#   1. Per-field assignment through a nested place, `(*c).ontologies
#      .entries[i].disjoint_count = ...`. Issue #1471: the store lands
#      somewhere else in the Checker. Whole-entry read-modify-write instead.
#      SEGV 30 -> 26 of 42.
#   2. One recursive frame per declared class overflowed the 8 MiB stack.
#      Measured: 18 classes OK, 19 SEGV, and BOTH pass under
#      `ulimit -s 524288`. The four list walks are iterative now.
#      SEGV 26 -> 0.
#   3. Single-pass superclass resolution against a list that parse_ontology_def
#      builds by PREPENDING, so it arrives in reverse source order and a
#      subclass is linked before its parent exists. The axiom was dropped in
#      silence. Measured with a probe build: `class Drug` then
#      `class Rapamycin subclass_of Drug` gave kaxioms=0, the same two
#      declarations in the other order gave kaxioms=1. Both walks resolve
#      names in a second pass now, which makes them order-independent.
#      OK 14 -> 41 of 42.
#
# Result: 41 of 42 ontology fixtures pass, against 40 of 42 on the committed
# baseline, and the one failure -- ontology_axiom_conjunction, refinement
# discharge from property axioms -- fails identically on that baseline.
#
# ANTI-VACUITY, because 40 of these were green while the subsystem did
# nothing: the wired checker was measured on rejection too. Subclass passed
# where the superclass is expected is accepted; superclass passed where the
# subclass is expected is rejected with E152; a disjointness violation is
# rejected. A green fixture list is not evidence on its own.
#
# STILL OPEN, stated rather than hidden: the by-value spine keeps the
# one-pass superclass resolution and therefore keeps defect 3. It is inert
# under Madaros, which is why the defect survived unnoticed in the first
# place. Fixing it is a separate measured change.

ItemAcquisitionPolicy
ItemAlternativeFrontier
ItemAlternativePolicy
ItemAudit
ItemCausalGraph
